An onion service carries a glowcap session over
[Tor](https://www.torproject.org/). A peer is named by a version 3 service id,
the 56 base32 characters in `ocapn://<serviceid>.onion`. Bytes travel a Tor
circuit: encrypted, with both ends' network location hidden.

This is the only transport the OCapN draft standardizes, and the only netlayer
upstream [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) ships. Every
other live Goblins check (cleartext `tcp`, and `iroh`) runs over an adapter
glowcap provides. Over Tor, each side uses its own implementation of the
draft transport, and `zig build goblins-onion` is the live check.

# A Tor you already run

glowcap talks to a Tor daemon you run. Give the netlayer two endpoints. Each is
a `host:port`, or in a binding, an absolute Unix socket path:

- the **SOCKS endpoint**, which glowcap dials through, and
- the **control endpoint**, which glowcap asks for a service when it hosts.

Without the control endpoint the netlayer can dial but not host, and it says so
rather than reporting an address nothing can reach. To authenticate to the
control endpoint, glowcap sends a cookie file's bytes or a password.

Hosting creates a version 3 onion service and reports the service id partners dial, plus
the key that restores that address after a restart. Store the key and pass it
back to keep one address across restarts.

# Dial

Point the netlayer at an already-running Tor SOCKS endpoint and name the
service id to reach.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:onion GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **Zig**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const glowcap = @import("glowcap");
>
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !void {
>     // Dial through a running Tor daemon's SOCKS port.
>     const transports: glowcap.driver.Transports = .{ .symbols = &.{.onion}, .onion = .{
>         .socks = try glowcap.net.tcp.parseDesignator("127.0.0.1:9050"),
>     } };
>     const partner = glowcap.net.onion.locator("<56-character onion service id>");
>     const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
>         allocator,
>         io,
>         transports,
>         partner,
>         .ephemeral,
>         .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
>     );
> ```
> **TypeScript**
> ```ts
> import { locator, provider } from "@glowcap/onion";
> import { Runtime } from "@glowcap/runtime";
>
> // glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> const partner = locator("<56-character onion service id>");
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	providers: [provider({ socks: "/run/tor/socks" })],
> });
> ```
> **Python**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, onion
>
> # glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> partner = onion.locator("<56-character onion service id>")
>
> async with Runtime.open(
>     provider=onion.provider(onion.OnionConfig(socks="/run/tor/socks"))
> ) as runtime:
> ```
> **Rust**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::onion::{self, Config};
> use glowcap::{Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> let partner = onion::locator("<56-character onion service id>");
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([onion::provider(
> 	Config::dial_only("/run/tor/socks"),
> )]))
> .await?;
> ```
> **Racket**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-onion.rkt")
>
> ;; glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> (define partner (onion-locator "<56-character onion service id>"))
> (define runtime
>   (open-runtime #:providers
>                 (list (onion-provider #:socks "/run/tor/socks"))))
> ```
